<p>Pharma medical affairs, payers, health systems and regulators all want the same thing from real-world data: evidence about what treatments actually do outside trials. The data, mostly claims and electronic health records, is plentiful. The hard part is design. Every observational study is an attempt to estimate a counterfactual, what would have happened to these patients without the exposure or with a different one, from data that only records what did happen<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<p>The OHDSI community implements five main designs for this as open-source R packages in its Methods Library, all running on OMOP data<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Knowing what each one assumes is the difference between evidence and a confident-looking chart.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>The five designs at a glance</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Design</th><th>Compares</th><th>Main strength</th><th>Main vulnerability</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Cohort method (new user)</td><td>New users of target vs new users of comparator</td><td>Emulates a trial; rich diagnostics</td><td>Unmeasured confounding between groups</td></tr>
<tr><td>Self-controlled cohort</td><td>Exposed time vs time just before exposure</td><td>No between-person confounding</td><td>Baseline risk differing between periods</td></tr>
<tr><td>Case-control</td><td>Exposure in people with vs without the outcome</td><td>Efficient for rare outcomes</td><td>Choice of controls</td></tr>
<tr><td>Case-crossover</td><td>Exposure at outcome time vs an earlier control time</td><td>Cases are their own controls</td><td>Trends in exposure over time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Self-controlled case series</td><td>Outcome rate in exposed vs all unexposed time</td><td>No between-person confounding</td><td>Time-varying confounding; outcomes that end observation</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>All rows are drawn from the descriptions in The Book of OHDSI<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Cohort method: emulating a trial</h2>
<p>The new-user cohort design compares people who start the target treatment with people who start a comparator, and follows them for a defined time-at-risk. It is defined by five choices: target cohort, comparator cohort, outcome cohort, time-at-risk and model<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/real-world-evidence-study-designs-omop/cohortMethod.webp" alt="Timeline diagram of the new-user cohort design: two subjects starting the target treatment and two starting the comparator, each with a covariate capture window before treatment start, an adjustment strategy between groups, and outcomes marked during treatment" width="1600" height="558" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The new-user cohort design: characteristics are captured before treatment start and used to adjust for differences between groups. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://ohdsi.github.io/TheBookOfOhdsi/PopulationLevelEstimation.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Book of OHDSI, Population-Level Estimation</a> · CC0 1.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>The central problem is that patients on different treatments differ. OHDSI's preferred adjustment is a propensity score fitted with large-scale regularised regression over many generic characteristics, typically 10,000 to 100,000 of them, rather than a hand-picked list<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The design comes with diagnostics: preference score distributions to check the groups overlap, and covariate balance after adjustment, where some guidelines recommend standardised differences below 0.1<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<p>The book is clear that this is inherently a comparative design. Comparing a treatment with no treatment is hard because a comparable unexposed group is difficult to define; an active comparator for the same indication is preferred<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Self-controlled designs: people as their own controls</h2>
<p>Three designs avoid between-person comparison altogether. The <strong>self-controlled cohort</strong> compares outcome rates during exposure with the time just before. The <strong>case-crossover</strong> design asks whether exposure was more common just before the outcome than at an earlier control time; because the outcome always comes later, it can be biased by exposure trends, which the case-time-control variant addresses. The <strong>self-controlled case series</strong> compares the outcome rate during exposed time with all unexposed time, before, between and after exposures<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/real-world-evidence-study-designs-omop/selfControlledCaseSeries.webp" alt="Timeline diagram of the self-controlled case series design: three subjects with alternating unexposed and target exposure periods, with outcomes marked in both exposed and unexposed time" width="1600" height="365" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The self-controlled case series: outcome rates during exposure are compared with all unexposed time in the same people. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://ohdsi.github.io/TheBookOfOhdsi/PopulationLevelEstimation.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Book of OHDSI, Population-Level Estimation</a> · CC0 1.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>These designs are robust to confounding by stable characteristics such as genetics or chronic conditions, but vulnerable to things that change over time, such as age, season or disease progression<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The self-controlled case series also assumes the end of observation is independent of the outcome, which can fail for fatal outcomes such as stroke; an extension exists to correct for that<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>No design is assumption-free. Choose the one whose weaknesses matter least for your question, then test it on questions where you already know the answer.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Case-control: efficient, but fragile</h2>
<p>Case-control studies compare exposure in people with the outcome against people without it, often matching on age and sex and sometimes nesting within a subgroup such as patients with the relevant indication<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. They are efficient for rare outcomes, but results depend heavily on how controls are selected.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How I'd choose and validate</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Write the question precisely</strong> as cohorts and time windows, as in <a href="/insights/omop-cohort-definitions/">OMOP cohort definitions</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Default to the new-user cohort design with an active comparator</strong> for comparative effectiveness, because its diagnostics make problems visible.</li>
<li><strong>Use self-controlled designs for acute outcomes after transient exposures</strong>, such as vaccine or short-course drug safety, where timing is sharp.</li>
<li><strong>Run negative controls.</strong> Outcomes believed not to be caused by the exposure reveal residual bias; the book's method validity chapter covers this and empirical calibration<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Replicate across databases</strong> through the <a href="/insights/federated-health-analytics-ohdsi/">federated network approach</a>, after <a href="/insights/omop-data-quality-checks/">data quality checks</a> at each site.</li>
<li><strong>Keep estimation and prediction separate.</strong> Causal questions and <a href="/insights/clinical-prediction-models-omop/">prediction models</a> need different designs and different validation.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How I can help</h2>
<p>I design OMOP platforms and analytics pipelines that make real-world evidence studies reproducible, diagnosable and fast to run across sites. See <a href="/healthcare-data-architecture/">healthcare data architecture</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
